Manual task publication and progress ownership
=============================================

Manual submission first reserves an organization-scoped progress row under a
new UUID using Redis SET NX with the existing 24-hour retention period. If the
reservation fails or the key already exists, the endpoint returns 503 and does
not call the publisher. The selected ID is passed explicitly to Celery. There
is no progress write after publication, so a fast worker completion cannot be
replaced with a pending row.

If publishing raises, delivery is uncertain: the broker may already have
accepted the message. The endpoint returns 503 with the task ID and instructions
to inspect it before retrying. It retains ownership and any worker result. It
does not delete the row, mark execution failed, or automatically publish again.
Storage and submission warnings omit connection exception text.

FreeSDNTask keeps cached progress and its start clock on the Celery request,
which separates nested and concurrent invocations of the reusable task object.
The startup hook preserves the seeded organization. Cached and stored progress
must match the requested task ID; a task payload's organization is not trusted
as monitoring authority. Retry progress remains in the owner's active list.
The existing API organization checks continue to hide other organizations and
unscoped tasks. Redis/broker access must remain restricted to trusted services.

Limits
------
This is not a durable outbox, exactly-once delivery, a task-idempotency protocol,
or reconciliation after broker/database/worker crashes. Expired or unavailable
progress can make a completed task unavailable through the tenant API. A
prepared row may remain pending until its TTL expires if no worker receives the
message. Operators must inspect uncertain submissions before manually retrying.
Duplicate deliveries with the same ID are not serialized by this change.

Celery SUCCESS still means that a function returned normally. Application
results such as {"success": false} must be interpreted separately; generic
business-outcome classification is not changed here. Worker exception hooks,
Celery's own logs and its result backend still require a separate diagnostic
privacy review. This change does not sanitize arbitrary task return values or
progress messages. A monitoring owner does not constrain a global scheduled
sweep; existing manual trigger authorization and argument checks are preserved.

Qualification
-------------
The regressions cover request isolation, actual eager Celery lifecycle hooks,
owner retention, fast completion, uncertain acknowledgements, active retries,
reservation refusal and storage diagnostics. In-memory transport tests are not
a substitute for the separately recorded isolated Valkey and worker checks.
No external hardware action, email, SMS or webhook is part of these fixtures.
